<h2>The text and its translation</h2>
<div class="texte-latin">
    <div class="entete lat">Latin</div>
    <div class="entete fr">English</div>
    <div class="lat">Lux fulgebit hodie super nos&nbsp;:</div>
    <div class="fr">A light shall shine upon us today&nbsp;;</div>
    <div class="lat">quia natus est nobis Dominus&nbsp;:</div>
    <div class="fr">for a Lord is born to us&nbsp;;</div>
    <div class="lat">et vocabitur Admirabilis, Deus,<br>Princeps pacis, Pater futuri sæculi&nbsp;:</div>
    <div class="fr">and he shall be called Wonderful, God,<br>Prince of peace, Father of the world to come&nbsp;;</div>
    <div class="lat">cujus regni non erit finis.</div>
    <div class="fr">and his reign shall have no end.</div>
</div>

<h2>The real subject here&nbsp;: the duration of the notes</h2>
<p class="lettrine">
    So far we have asked «&nbsp;how many notes&nbsp;?&nbsp;» and «&nbsp;do they go up or
    down&nbsp;?&nbsp;». <span class="mot-latin">Lux fulgebit</span> invites us to a subtler
    question&nbsp;: «&nbsp;how <em>long</em> does each note last&nbsp;?&nbsp;». For in
    chant, not all notes have the same duration&nbsp;: some are light and fleeting, others
    settle and broaden.
</p>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Definition — short note, lengthened note</span>
    <p>
        Chant alternates <strong>short</strong> notes (the ordinary, light flow) and
        <strong>lengthened</strong> notes, on which the voice lingers for a moment. This is
        not a «&nbsp;square&nbsp;» rhythm like a military march&nbsp;: it is a supple
        breathing, guided by the meaning of the text.
    </p>
</div>

<h3>Where do you read these durations&nbsp;?</h3>
<p>This is precisely the area where the old notations of the <em>Triplex</em> shine&nbsp;:</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>The episema</strong> (small stroke) above a St. Gall neume&nbsp;:
        «&nbsp;settle, broaden&nbsp;».
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>The letter <span class="mot-latin">t</span></strong> (<span
        class="mot-latin">tenete</span>)&nbsp;: «&nbsp;hold the note&nbsp;».
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>The letter <span class="mot-latin">c</span></strong> (<span
        class="mot-latin">celeriter</span>)&nbsp;: on the contrary, «&nbsp;pass quickly, do
        not lean&nbsp;».
    </li>
    <li>
        In the notation of <strong>Laon</strong>, the very shape of the stroke (rounded,
        «&nbsp;hooked&nbsp;»…) already distinguishes short from long with remarkable
        precision.
    </li>
</ul>

<div class="encadre astuce">
    <span class="etiquette">A very simple exercise</span>
    <p>
        On the word <span class="mot-latin">Lux</span> (the «&nbsp;light&nbsp;») and then on
        <span class="mot-latin">fulgebit</span> («&nbsp;will shine&nbsp;»), ask
        yourself&nbsp;: does the upper layer (Laon) or the lower one (St. Gall) tell me to
        <em>settle</em> the voice, or to <em>flow on</em>&nbsp;? You will find that the
        moments of leaning often fall on the words the text wants to highlight&nbsp;: the
        light, the birth, the peace.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Seeing the three notations</h2>
<div class="facsimile">
    <p>📜 <strong>Space for the facsimile of «&nbsp;Lux fulgebit&nbsp;»</strong></p>
    <p>
Ideally, circle in colour the episemas and the letters <span class="mot-latin">t</span> and
<span class="mot-latin">c</span>, to make the durations visible at a glance.<br>
<code>&lt;img src="{{ asset('assets/img/lux-fulgebit.jpg') }}" alt="Lux fulgebit in the
Graduale Triplex"&gt;</code>
</p>
</div>

<div class="legende-triplex">
    <span><span class="pastille laon"></span>Laon, above&nbsp;: the shape of the stroke tells short/long</span>
    <span><span class="pastille vat"></span>Square notation, centre&nbsp;: the notes</span>
    <span><span class="pastille sg"></span>St. Gall, below&nbsp;: episemas, letters <em>t</em>/<em>c</em></span>
</div>

<div class="encadre">
    <span class="etiquette">Key point for this chant</span>
    <p>
        With <span class="mot-latin">Lux fulgebit</span> we move from the question
        «&nbsp;which notes&nbsp;?&nbsp;» to the question «&nbsp;what duration, what
        leaning&nbsp;?&nbsp;». This is the heart of what makes the <em>Graduale Triplex</em>
        so precious&nbsp;: the two old layers make audible what square notation alone leaves
        mute.
    </p>
</div>

